{"passage_id": "TWAIN_HUCKLEBERRY_FINN_012", "condition": "modernize", "rewritten_text": "Next Sunday, we all went to church, about three miles away, with everyone riding horses. The men brought their guns along, just like Buck did, and kept them between their knees or leaned them against the wall. The Shepherdsons did the same thing. The sermon was pretty dull, all about loving your brother and that kind of thing, but everyone said it was good and they all discussed it on the way home. They had a lot to say about faith, good deeds, and free will, and it seemed to me like one of the most boring Sundays I'd ever had.

About an hour after dinner, everyone was dozing off, some in their chairs and some in their rooms, and it got really quiet. Buck and a dog were lying on the grass in the sun, fast asleep. I went up to our room and thought I'd take a nap too. I found Miss Sophia standing in her doorway, which was next to ours, and she invited me into her room. She closed the door quietly and asked me if I liked her, and I said I did. Then she asked me if I'd do something for her without telling anyone, and I agreed. She said she'd forgotten her Bible at church, left it between two other books, and would I sneak out and get it for her without saying anything to anyone. I said I would.

So I slipped out and made my way up the road, and there was no one at the church except maybe a few pigs, since there was no lock on the door and pigs like to hang out on the wooden floor in the summer because it's cool. If you notice, most people only go to church when they have to, but pigs are different. I thought to myself, something's going on; it's not normal for a girl to be so worried about a Bible. So I shook it, and a small piece of paper fell out with '2:30' written on it in pencil. I searched the Bible but couldn't find anything else. I didn't understand what the paper meant, so I put it back in the Bible. When I got home and upstairs, Miss Sophia was waiting for me in her doorway. She pulled me in and closed the door, then she looked in the Bible until she found the paper. As soon as she read it, she looked happy, and before I knew it, she grabbed me and gave me a hug, saying I was the best boy in the world and not to tell anyone. She was really flushed for a minute, and her eyes lit up, making her look really pretty. I was pretty surprised, but when I caught my breath, I asked her what the paper was about. She asked me if I'd read it, and I said no. She asked me if I could read writing, and I told her I could only read print. Then she said the paper was just a bookmark to keep her place, and I could go play now."}

{"passage_id": "WILDE_DORIAN_GRAY_018", "condition": "modernize", "rewritten_text": "As morning breaks, white fingers of light creep through the curtains, and they seem to tremble. Dark, fantastical shapes - silent shadows - crawl into the corners of the room and huddle there. Outside, the sounds of birds chirping among the leaves, people heading to work, or the wind sighing and sobbing as it comes down from the hills and circles the silent house, are all audible. It's as if the wind is hesitant to wake the sleepers, yet it must rouse sleep from its deep, purple slumber. Veil after veil of thin, dusky gauze lifts, and gradually, the forms and colors of things are restored to them. We watch as dawn recreates the world in its timeless pattern. The pale mirrors regain their reflective life. The unlit candles stand where we had left them, and beside them lies the half-read book we had been studying, or the wired flower we had worn to the ball, or the letter we had been afraid to read, or the one we had read too many times. Nothing appears to have changed. The real life we knew returns from the unreal shadows of the night, and we must resume it where we left off. A terrible sense of the necessity for continuing our routine, with its monotonous, stereotyped habits, washes over us. Alternatively, we may feel a wild longing for our eyes to open one morning to a world that has been transformed in the darkness, a world with fresh shapes and colors, where things would be changed, or hold new secrets. In this world, the past would have little or no place, or would survive only as a subconscious memory, without the burden of obligation or regret. Even the recollection of joy would be bitter, and memories of pleasure would be painful.

For Dorian Gray, creating such worlds seemed to be the true purpose of life. In his pursuit of new and exciting sensations, with their essential element of strangeness that fuels romance, he would often adopt certain ways of thinking that he knew were not truly his own. He would surrender to their subtle influences, absorb their essence, and then, having satisfied his intellectual curiosity, leave them behind with a curious indifference. This indifference is not incompatible with a genuine ardor of temperament and, according to some modern psychologists, is often a condition of it.

There was a rumor that Dorian Gray was going to join the Roman Catholic Church, and it's true that the Roman ritual had always fascinated him. The daily sacrifice, more awe-inspiring than any ancient sacrifice, moved him deeply. It was the ritual's rejection of sensory evidence, its primitive simplicity, and the eternal pathos of the human tragedy it symbolized that drew him in. He loved to kneel on the cold marble floor and watch the priest, in his ornate, flower-patterned dalmatic, slowly lift the veil of the tabernacle or raise the jeweled, lantern-shaped monstrance with the pale wafer that, at times, seemed to be the true 'bread of angels.' He was also captivated by the priest, robed in the garments of Christ's Passion, breaking the Host into the chalice and striking his breast in penance for his sins."}

