{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_001",
  "condition": "paraphrase",
  "rewritten_text": "As she gazed into those soft blue eyes, engaged in conversation, and devised various plans, the evening slipped away at an extraordinary pace. Before she knew it, the supper table, which typically marked the end of such gatherings, was set and ready, and had been moved to the fire. With an enthusiasm that surpassed her usual diligence, and a genuine eagerness to please, she attended to the needs of her guests, recommending the minced chicken and scalloped oysters with a zeal that she knew would be appreciated by their considerate visitors. Mr. Woodhouse's feelings, however, were torn. He had a fondness for the traditional supper, but his conviction that it was unwholesome caused him distress. While he wanted to welcome his guests with open arms, his concern for their health made him regret the abundance of food. All he could wholeheartedly recommend was a small basin of thin gruel, similar to his own. Nevertheless, as the ladies enjoyed the finer dishes, he managed to suggest, 'Mrs. Bates, might I propose you try one of these eggs? A softly boiled egg is not harmful. Serle is particularly skilled at boiling eggs, and I would not recommend anyone else's. But please, do not be afraid, they are quite small. Miss Bates, allow Emma to serve you a tiny portion of tart - just a little. Our tarts are all made with apples, and you need not worry about unwholesome preserves. I would advise against the custard, though. Mrs. Goddard, would you like half a glass of wine, diluted with water, of course?' Emma humored her father, but attended to her guests' needs in a far more satisfactory manner. On this particular evening, she took great pleasure in seeing them depart happy. Miss Smith's joy was equal to Emma's intentions. As a prominent figure in Highbury, Emma's introduction had initially caused Miss Smith both excitement and trepidation. However, the young girl left with deeply gratified feelings, delighted by Emma's affability throughout the evening, and even shaken hands with her before departing. Harriet Smith soon became a regular visitor to Hartfield. Emma, being decisive and prompt, lost no time in inviting her, encouraging her to visit often, and as their acquaintance grew, so did their mutual satisfaction. Emma had foreseen the benefits of having Harriet as a walking companion, a role that had become more important since Mrs. Weston's departure. Emma's father rarely ventured beyond the shrubbery, where two sections of the grounds were sufficient for his walks, regardless of the season. With Mrs. Weston no longer present, Emma found Harriet's company to be a welcome addition."
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_001",
  "condition": "simplify",
  "rewritten_text": "Emma was so caught up in admiring the soft blue eyes of her guest, talking and listening, and coming up with plans, that the evening flew by quickly. Before she knew it, the supper table was set and ready, and had been moved to the fire. With her usual enthusiasm and good intentions, Emma made sure everything was done well and that her guests felt welcome. She helped serve the food, recommending the minced chicken and scalloped oysters, knowing that her guests would appreciate her efforts. Mr. Woodhouse, Emma's father, was torn. He liked the tradition of having a supper, but he was concerned about the health effects of eating late at night. He wanted his guests to feel welcome, but he also wanted to make sure they didn't overeat. He suggested that they try some thin gruel, which he thought was a healthier option. As the ladies were eating, he said, 'Mrs. Bates, why don't you try one of these eggs? They're boiled very soft and aren't unwholesome. Our cook, Serle, does a great job boiling eggs.' He also offered Miss Bates a small piece of tart, saying, 'Just a little bit, it's apple tart and the preserves are wholesome.' He even offered Mrs. Goddard a small amount of wine, mixed with water. Emma let her father talk, but she made sure her guests had enough to eat and drink. She was happy to see them enjoying themselves, especially Miss Smith, who had been a bit nervous about meeting her. As the evening came to a close, Emma was pleased to see her guests leaving happy. Miss Smith was particularly grateful for the kind way Emma had treated her. As they said their goodbyes, Emma invited Miss Smith to come back and visit again soon. Over time, Miss Smith became a regular visitor to Hartfield, and she and Emma grew closer. Emma had been looking for a walking companion, and Miss Smith fit the bill perfectly. Since Mrs. Weston was no longer around, Emma's father didn't have anyone to walk with, and he rarely went beyond the shrubbery. Emma was happy to have someone to share her walks with, and she and Miss Smith quickly became good friends."
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_002",
  "condition": "simplify",
  "rewritten_text": "When Emma was fourteen, she made a list of books to read - I remember thinking it showed good judgment, so I kept it for a while. I'm sure she could make a good list now, but I don't expect her to do much steady reading. She won't do anything that requires effort and patience, and she won't listen to reason. Miss Taylor couldn't get her to read much, and I don't think Harriet Smith will be able to either. You know you could never get Emma to read as much as you wanted her to. 'I dare say,' said Mrs. Weston with a smile, 'that I thought so at the time, but since we parted, I don't remember Emma ever not doing something I wanted her to.' 'There's hardly any point in remembering something like that,' said Mr. Knightley, 'but I, who haven't been charmed by her, still have to see, hear, and remember. Emma is spoiled because she's the cleverest person in her family. At ten years old, she could answer questions that puzzled her seventeen-year-old sister. She's always been quick and confident, while her sister Isabella is slow and unsure. Since she was twelve, Emma has been in charge of the house and everyone in it. She lost the one person who could have controlled her when her mother died. Emma inherited her mother's talents and would have been under her control if she were still alive. 'I would have been sorry to rely on your recommendation if I had left the Woodhouse family and needed a new job,' said Mrs. Weston. 'I don't think you would have said anything good about me to anyone. I'm sure you always thought I wasn't suited for the job I had.' 'Yes,' said Mr. Knightley with a smile, 'you're better suited as a wife than a governess. But you were learning how to be a good wife while you were at Hartfield. You may not have given Emma the best education, but you were learning from her how to submit to your husband and do what you're told. If Weston had asked me to recommend a wife for him, I would have suggested you.' 'Thank you,' said Mrs. Weston. 'It won't be hard to be a good wife to someone like Mr. Weston.' 'To be honest, I think you're wasting your potential, and you might not have anything to deal with. But we won't give up hope. Maybe Weston will become grumpy because he's too comfortable, or his son will cause him trouble.' 'I hope not,' said Mrs. Weston. 'It's not likely. No, Mr. Knightley, don't predict problems from that quarter.' 'I'm not saying it will happen,' said Mr. Knightley. 'I'm just mentioning possibilities. I don't claim to have Emma's ability to predict the future. I hope the young man will be as good as a Weston and as wealthy as a Churchill. But Harriet Smith - I haven't even started talking about her. I think she's the worst possible companion for Emma."
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_003",
  "condition": "paraphrase",
  "rewritten_text": "You need not be prompted to express sorrow for his disappointment, Harriet was told. 'You think I ought to refuse him then,' said Harriet, looking down. 'Ought to refuse him! My dear Harriet, what do you mean? Are you in any doubt as to that? I thought - but I beg your pardon, perhaps I have been under a mistake. I certainly have been misunderstanding you, if you feel in doubt as to the purport of your answer. I had imagined you were consulting me only as to the wording of it.' Harriet was silent. With a little reserve of manner, Emma continued, 'You mean to return a favourable answer, I collect.' 'No, I do not; that is, I do not mean - What shall I do? What would you advise me to do? Pray, dear Miss Woodhouse, tell me what I ought to do.' 'I shall not give you any advice, Harriet. I will have nothing to do with it. This is a point which you must settle with your feelings.' 'I had no notion that he liked me so very much,' said Harriet, contemplating the letter. For a little while Emma persevered in her silence; but beginning to apprehend the bewitching flattery of that letter might be too powerful, she thought it best to say, 'I lay it down as a general rule, Harriet, that if a woman doubts as to whether she should accept a man or not, she certainly ought to refuse him. If she can hesitate as to 'yes,' she ought to say 'no' directly. It is not a state to be safely entered into with doubtful feelings, with half a heart. I thought it my duty as a friend, and older than yourself, to say thus much to you. But do not imagine that I want to influence you.' 'Oh! no, I am sure you are a great deal too kind to - but if you would just advise me what I had best do - No, no, I do not mean that - As you say, one's mind ought to be quite made up - One should not be hesitating - It is a very serious thing. - It will be safer to say 'no,' perhaps. - Do you think I had better say 'no'?' 'Not for the world,' said Emma, smiling graciously, 'would I advise you either way. You must be the best judge of your own happiness. If you prefer Mr. Martin to every other person; if you think him the most agreeable man you have ever been in company with, why should you hesitate? You blush, Harriet. - Does any body else occur to you at this moment under such a definition? Harriet, Harriet, do not deceive yourself; do not be run away with by gratitude and compassion. At this moment whom are you thinking of?' The symptoms were favourable. - Instead of answering, Harriet turned away confused, and stood thoughtfully by the fire; and though the letter was still in her hand, it was now mechanically twisted about without regard. Emma waited the result with impatience, but not without strong hopes. At last, with some hesitation, Harriet said, 'Miss Woodhouse, as you will not give me your opinion, I must do as well as I can by myself; and I have now quite determined, and really almost made up my mind - to refuse Mr. Martin.'"
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_003",
  "condition": "simplify",
  "rewritten_text": "You don't need to be told to look sad about his disappointment, Harriet said, looking down. 'You think I should refuse him then,' she added. Emma replied, 'My dear Harriet, what do you mean? Are you unsure about what to do? I thought you were just asking about how to word your answer, not whether to accept or refuse him.' Harriet was silent, and Emma continued with a bit of reserve, 'You mean to say yes, I assume.' Harriet responded, 'No, I don't know - what should I do? Please, Miss Woodhouse, tell me what you think I should do.' Emma said, 'I won't give you any advice, Harriet. This is something you must decide for yourself, based on your own feelings.' Harriet said, 'I had no idea he liked me so much,' as she looked at the letter. Emma stayed quiet for a moment, but then she spoke up, 'As a general rule, Harriet, if a woman is unsure about whether to accept a man, she should probably say no. If she's not sure, it's not a good idea to get married with doubts. I'm only saying this because I care about you and I'm older than you.' Harriet said, 'Oh, no, you're too kind - but if you could just tell me what you think I should do...' Emma smiled and said, 'I won't advise you either way. You have to decide what makes you happy. If you think Mr. Martin is the most agreeable man you've met, then why are you hesitating? You're blushing, Harriet - is someone else on your mind?' Harriet turned away, looking confused, and stood by the fire, twisting the letter in her hand. Emma waited to see what Harriet would do, and after a moment, Harriet said, 'Miss Woodhouse, since you won't give me your opinion, I'll have to make up my own mind. And I've almost decided to refuse Mr. Martin.'"
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_004",
  "condition": "simplify",
  "rewritten_text": "At seventeen, a girl is just starting her life and people are beginning to notice her. It's not surprising that she doesn't accept the first marriage proposal she gets. 'I've always thought it was a bad idea for you to be so close to Harriet,' said Mr. Knightley. 'I've kept my thoughts to myself until now, but I think it will be bad for Harriet. You'll give her a big head and make her think she's entitled to more than she is. This will cause all sorts of problems. It's easy for a young woman to have high expectations, but Miss Harriet Smith might not get many marriage proposals, even though she's pretty. Smart men don't want silly wives, and men from good families don't want to marry someone with a mysterious past. If she marries Robert Martin, she'll be safe and happy, but if you encourage her to aim high, she might end up living with Mrs. Goddard forever, or at least until she gets desperate and marries someone she doesn't love. 'We disagree on this, Mr. Knightley,' said Emma. 'There's no point in discussing it further. As for Harriet marrying Robert Martin, it's impossible - she's already turned him down and I don't think he'll ask her again. She'll have to live with the consequences of rejecting him. I might have influenced her decision a little, but there wasn't much I could do. Robert Martin isn't very attractive and his manners are bad, so even if Harriet was interested in him before, she's not now. She's met better men and knows what a real gentleman is. 'That's nonsense,' said Mr. Knightley. 'Robert Martin is a sensible and sincere person, and his good manners are more than Harriet could understand.' Emma didn't respond and tried to look calm, but she was feeling uncomfortable and wanted Mr. Knightley to leave."
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_005",
  "condition": "simplify",
  "rewritten_text": "He is a very clever boy, and all his siblings are remarkably clever too. They have many pretty ways and will often come to me and ask for things like a bit of string. One time, Henry asked me for a knife, but I told him knives are only for grandfapas. I think their father can be too rough with them sometimes. He appears rough to me because I am very gentle, but if I could compare him to other fathers, I would not think him rough. He wants his boys to be active and hardy, and if they misbehave, he can give them a sharp word now and then. However, he is an affectionate father, and the children are all fond of him. When their uncle comes in, he tosses them up to the ceiling in a way that seems frightening. But they like it, and it's something they enjoy so much that if their uncle didn't make them take turns, one of them would never give way to the other. I don't understand it, but that's how it is with everyone - one half of the world can't understand the pleasures of the other. Later that morning, just as the girls were about to separate for dinner, the man who was the hero of the charade walked in again. Harriet turned away, but Emma could smile and talk to him. She noticed that he seemed conscious of having taken a risk and was waiting to see how it would turn out. He said he wanted to know if he was needed at Hartfield that evening, or if he could go to his friend Cole's house for dinner instead. Emma thanked him but told him he shouldn't disappoint his friend, and her father was looking forward to playing cards with him. He asked again, but she said no again. Then, he seemed about to leave, but Emma stopped him and gave him back the paper with the charade on it. She said, 'Oh, here is the charade you left with us. Thank you for letting us see it. We liked it so much that I've written it into Miss Smith's collection. I hope your friend won't mind.' Mr. Elton didn't know what to say and looked confused. He said something about honor and looked at Emma and Harriet, then picked up the book and examined it carefully. Emma smiled and said, 'You must apologize to your friend for me, but a good charade like this should be shared with everyone. He can be sure that all the women will like it because he writes with such gallantry.'"
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_006",
  "condition": "simplify",
  "rewritten_text": "Papa is only expressing his own regret, said Emma. 'Very much to the honour of both,' was the handsome reply. Isabella asked her father, 'And do you see her, sir, often?' in a plaintive tone that suited him. Mr. Woodhouse hesitated, 'Not near so often, my dear, as I could wish.' Emma added, 'Oh, papa, we have missed seeing them only one entire day since they married. Either in the morning or evening of every day, excepting one, we have seen either Mr. Weston or Mrs. Weston, and generally both, either at Randalls or here - and as you may suppose, Isabella, most frequently here.' She continued, 'They are very kind in their visits. Mr. Weston is really as kind as herself. Papa, if you speak in that melancholy way, you will be giving Isabella a false idea of us all. Every body must be aware that Miss Taylor must be missed, but every body ought also to be assured that Mr. and Mrs. Weston do really prevent our missing her by any means to the extent we ourselves anticipated - which is the exact truth.' Mr. John Knightley said, 'Just as it should be, and just as I hoped it was from your letters. Her wish of showing you attention could not be doubted, and his being a disengaged and social man makes it all easy.' He told his wife, 'I have been always telling you, my love, that I had no idea of the change being so very material to Hartfield as you apprehended; and now you have Emma's account, I hope you will be satisfied.' Mr. Woodhouse said, 'Why, to be sure - yes, certainly - I cannot deny that Mrs. Weston, poor Mrs. Weston, does come and see us pretty often - but then - she is always obliged to go away again.' Emma replied, 'It would be very hard upon Mr. Weston if she did not, papa. You quite forget poor Mr. Weston.' John Knightley said pleasantly, 'I think, indeed, that Mr. Weston has some little claim. You and I, Emma, will venture to take the part of the poor husband.' He joked, 'I, being a husband, and you not being a wife, the claims of the man may very likely strike us with equal force.' Isabella heard this and said, 'Me, my love - are you talking about me? I am sure nobody ought to be, or can be, a greater advocate for matrimony than I am; and if it had not been for the misery of her leaving Hartfield, I should never have thought of Miss Taylor but as the most fortunate woman in the world.' She praised Mr. Weston, saying, 'I think there is nothing he does not deserve. I believe he is one of the very best-tempered men that ever existed. Excepting yourself and your brother, I do not know his equal for temper.' She remembered, 'I shall never forget his flying Henry's kite for him that very windy day last Easter - and ever since his particular kindness last September twelvemonth in writing that note, at twelve o'clock at night, on purpose to assure me that there was no scarlet fever at Cobham, I have been convinced there could not be a more feeling heart nor a better man in existence.'"
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_007",
  "condition": "simplify",
  "rewritten_text": "You are doing the right thing, she said to herself, we will make your apologies to Mr. and Mrs. Weston. But before she could say anything else, her brother offered Mr. Elton a seat in his carriage, saying that if the weather was the only thing stopping him, he would be happy to have him along. Mr. Elton accepted the offer and seemed very pleased with himself. He had never looked happier, and his smile was stronger than ever when he looked at her next. She thought to herself, this is strange. After I managed to get him to leave Harriet alone, he chooses to go out and leave her behind. It's very strange. But she believed that many men, especially single ones, had a strong desire to go out and dine with others. For them, having a dinner invitation was a big deal, and they would do anything to keep those engagements. This must be how Mr. Elton felt, she thought. He was a kind and pleasant young man who was in love with Harriet, but he couldn't resist a dinner invitation. It was odd how love worked - he could see the good qualities in Harriet, but he wouldn't give up dining out for her. Soon after, Mr. Elton left, and she had to admit that he seemed very sincere when he said goodbye. He promised to visit Harriet soon and hoped to have good news to share with her. He sighed and smiled as he left, which made her think more positively of him. After a few minutes of silence, John Knightley started talking. I have never seen a man try so hard to be nice as Mr. Elton, he said. When he's around ladies, he works very hard to be charming. With men, he can be normal and unaffected, but with ladies, he tries too hard. Mr. Elton's manners are not perfect, Emma replied, but when someone is trying to be nice, you should overlook their flaws. If a man is doing his best with the abilities he has, he will be more liked than someone who is superior but doesn't try. Mr. Elton has a very good temperament and is always willing to please, which is something to appreciate. Yes, John Knightley said with a hint of slyness, he seems to have a lot of good feelings towards you. Me, she replied with a surprised smile, are you thinking that Mr. Elton is interested in me. The idea had crossed his mind, he admitted, and he thought she should consider it. Mr. Elton in love with me, what an idea, she thought. I'm not saying it's true, John Knightley said, but you should think about it and behave accordingly. I think you've been encouraging him."
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_008",
  "condition": "paraphrase",
  "rewritten_text": "He had proceeded beyond the sweep, some way along the Highbury road, where the snow was nowhere more than half an inch deep and in many places barely enough to whiten the ground. Only a few flakes were falling at present, but the clouds were parting, and it seemed the snow would soon cease. He had spoken with the coachmen, and they shared his opinion that there was nothing to fear. This news brought great relief to Isabella, and it was also welcome to Emma, as it eased her father's concerns. However, her father's nervous nature meant he could not be completely at ease while still at Randalls. Although he believed it was safe to return home, he was convinced it was not safe to stay. While the others discussed and debated, Mr. Knightley and Emma quickly settled the matter: 'Your father will not be easy; why do not you go?' 'I am ready, if the others are.' 'Shall I ring the bell?' 'Yes, do.' The bell was rung, and the carriages were ordered. Emma hoped that soon one troublesome companion would be taken home to sober up and the other would recover his temper once this difficult visit was over. The carriage arrived, and Mr. Woodhouse was carefully helped into his own carriage by Mr. Knightley and Mr. Weston. Despite their reassurances, Mr. Woodhouse's anxiety was renewed at the sight of the snow and the darker night than he had expected. He feared they would have a bad drive, that Isabella would not like it, and that Emma would be uncomfortable in the carriage behind. He instructed James to drive slowly and wait for the other carriage. Isabella got into the carriage after her father, and John Knightley, forgetting he was not part of their group, naturally followed his wife. As a result, Emma found herself escorted into the second carriage by Mr. Elton, and they were to have a private drive. Previously, this would have been a pleasure, and she could have talked to him about Harriet, making the three-quarters of a mile seem short. But now, she would have preferred it not to happen. She suspected Mr. Elton had drunk too much of Mr. Weston's wine and would likely talk nonsense. To restrain him with her own behavior, she prepared to speak calmly and seriously about the weather and the night. However, they had barely passed the sweep-gate and joined the other carriage when she found her topic interrupted, her hand taken, and her attention demanded by Mr. Elton."
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_008",
  "condition": "simplify",
  "rewritten_text": "He had walked some way along the Highbury road, past the sweep. The snow was not deep, only about half an inch in some places, and hardly visible in others. A few flakes were still falling, but the clouds were clearing, and it seemed the snow would soon stop. He had spoken to the coachmen, and they agreed with him that there was nothing to worry about. Isabella was very relieved to hear this, and Emma was also glad, as it eased her father's worries. He was a nervous man and had been anxious about the weather. However, even with the good news, he couldn't relax while he was at Randalls. He thought it was safe to go home, but he was not convinced it was safe to stay. Mr. Knightley and Emma quickly decided that he should leave. 'Your father will not be easy; why do not you go?' Mr. Knightley asked. 'I am ready, if the others are,' Emma replied. 'Shall I ring the bell?' 'Yes, do,' she said. The bell was rung, and the carriages were called for. Emma hoped that soon she would see one of her troublesome companions go home to sober up, and the other recover his temper. The carriage arrived, and Mr. Woodhouse was helped in by Mr. Knightley and Mr. Weston. But even with their reassurances, he was still worried when he saw the snow and the dark night. 'He was afraid they would have a bad drive. He was afraid poor Isabella would not like it. And there would be poor Emma in the carriage behind. He did not know what they had best do. They must keep as much together as they could,' he said. James was instructed to drive slowly and wait for the other carriage. Isabella got in after her father, and John Knightley followed her, which meant Emma found herself alone with Mr. Elton in the second carriage. This would have been pleasant earlier in the day, but now she would rather it had not happened. She thought Mr. Elton had had too much wine and would talk nonsense. To avoid this, she prepared to speak calmly and seriously about the weather. But before she could start, Mr. Elton took her hand and demanded her attention."
}

{
